WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:12.620
The grammar school boy from Stratford-upon-Avon has landed a scholarly punch after groundbreaking research showed that Shakespeare does benefit children’s literacy and emotional development.
一项开创性研究表明，莎士比亚确实有益于儿童的读写能力和情感发展，这位来自埃文河畔斯特拉特福的文法学校男孩由此在学术上一击命中。

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.990
But only if you act him out.
但前提是要把他的戏演出来。

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:25.400
A study found that a “rehearsal room” approach to teaching Shakespeare broadened children’s vocabulary and the complexity of their writing as well as their emotional literacy.
一项研究发现，用“排练室”方法教莎士比亚，扩大了孩子们的词汇量，提高了他们写作的复杂程度，也提升了他们的情感素养。

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:39.400
“The research shows that the way actors work makes a big difference to the way children use language and also how they think about themselves,” Jacqui O’Hanlon of the Royal Shakespeare Company (RSC), which commissioned the study, said.
委托开展这项研究的皇家莎士比亚剧团（RSC）的杰姬·奥汉隆说：“研究表明，演员的工作方式对孩子们使用语言的方式，以及他们如何看待自己，都有很大影响。”

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:51.980
The randomised controlled trial involved hundreds of year 5 pupils—aged nine and ten—at 45 state primary schools that had not been “previously exposed to RSC pedagogy”.
这项随机对照试验的参与者是45所公立小学的数百名五年级学生，年龄在九岁到十岁之间；这些学校此前没有“接触过皇家莎士比亚剧团的教学法”。

00:00:52.780 --> 00:01:01.620
They were split into target and control groups and asked to write, for example, a message in a bottle as Ferdinand following the shipwreck in The Tempest.
学生们被分成目标组和对照组，并按要求写作，比如以《暴风雨》中费迪南德的身份，在海难之后写一封瓶中信。

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:07.580
The target group were given a 30-minute drama-based activity to accompany the passage.
研究者为目标组安排了一项时长30分钟、以戏剧为基础的活动，配合这段文字进行。

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:18.570
The peer-reviewed results showed that the target group of pupils drew on a wider vocabulary, used words “classed as more sophisticated or rarer”, and wrote at greater length.
经同行评审的研究结果显示，目标组的学生调用的词汇范围更广，用上了“归为更高级或更罕见”的词语，写得也更长。

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:33.350
They also “appear to be more comfortable writing in role…while [control] pupils imagine how they themselves would react to being shipwrecked, [target] children put themselves in the shoes of a literary character and express that character’s emotion”.
他们还“似乎更能自如地以角色身份写作……［对照组］学生想象的是他们自己遭遇海难会作何反应，而［目标组］孩子则设身处地站在文学人物的角度，表达这个人物的情感”。

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:50.950
The Time to Act study, which is published by the RSC this week, also found that while control pupils relied on “desert island clichés” such as palm trees, target pupils were “more expansive, [giving] a broader picture of the sky, the sea and the atmospheric conditions”.
皇家莎士比亚剧团本周发布的这项“该表演了”（Time to Act）研究还发现，对照组学生依赖棕榈树之类的“荒岛俗套”，而目标组学生则“写得更舒展，［呈现出］一幅关于天空、大海和大气状况的更广阔图景”。

00:01:51.750 --> 00:02:01.870
O’Hanlon said she had been most surprised by the “emotional literacy that was evident in the [target] children’s writing” and that they were “more resilient in their writing, more hopeful”.
奥汉隆说，最让她惊讶的是“［目标组］孩子们的作文中明显流露出的情感素养”，而且他们“在写作中更坚韧，更充满希望”。

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:12.470
She added: “The emotional understanding was very evident and it is probably related to the [rehearsal room process] where you are used to trying to imagine your way through.
她补充说：“对情感的理解体现得非常明显，这很可能与［排练室流程］有关，在这一过程中，你习惯了尝试凭借想象一步步摸索前行。

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:20.310
They were comfortable in describing different emotional states and part of what you do in drama is put yourself in different shoes.”
他们能自如地描述各种不同的情绪状态，而你在戏剧中要做的事，有一部分就是设身处地，站到不同人的立场上去。”

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:25.200
The study showed the importance of embedding arts in education, she said.
她说，这项研究表明了将艺术融入教育的重要性。

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:29.270
But could the results be replicated with any old dramatist?
但换成随便哪位剧作家，这些结果能否复现呢？

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:46.990
O’Hanlon said more research would be needed but suggested that Shakespeare’s use of 20,000 words, compared with the everyday 2,000 words, gave a “massive expansion of language into children’s lives”, which was combined with children “using their whole bodies to bring words to life”.
奥汉隆说，这还需要更多研究，但她表示，莎士比亚用了20,000个词，而日常用语只有2,000个词，这让“语言大规模地扩展到孩子们的生活中”，而这一点又与孩子们“用整个身体让文字活起来”结合在一起。
